overnments or hidden fees. That's the world Bitcoin promised when it was born out of chaos in 2008 during a global financial meltdown. Bitcoin was introduced by a mysterious figure called Satoshi Nakamoto. No one knows who he is, yet his invention sparked a financial revolution that defies borders and institutions. So why was Bitcoin created? Because people had lost trust, banks collapsed, governments printed money and the average person suffered. Bitcoin became the alternative, a currency built on math. Bitcoin is decentralized. No one controls it, not your bank, not your government. It's powered by thousands of computers across the world. It's like the Internet of money. Every Bitcoin transaction is stored on a blockchain, a public permanent digital ledger. Anyone can verify it, but no one can change it. Unlike cash, Bitcoin has a fixed supply. Only 21 million coins will ever exist. This scarcity is what gives it value like digital gold. Mining is how new Bitcoins are created. It's like solving complex math puzzles. Once solved, the miner earns a reward in Bitcoin. People use Bitcoin for different reasons, some to avoid inflation, others for privacy and some just to invest. It's freedom wrapped in code. But Bitcoin isn't perfect. It's slow, uses energy and isn't ideal for daily purchases. That's why other cryptocurrencies started emerging. Ethereum, for example, does more than money. It lets people create apps, games and contracts, all decentralized. Think of it as the programmable Internet. Let's not forget wallets. A crypto wallet stores your keys, your access to your funds. Lose them and your crypto is gone forever. Public key and private key, they sound complicated, but it's like having a username and a password. Your public key is for receiving, private key for spending. Crypto exchanges are where people buy and sell coins. Some are centralized like banks, others decentralized peer to peer. Each has pros and cons. What gives Bitcoin value? Trust, scarcity and